\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Step 1: The contract (derived and tested)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Number tokens from t = 0 and let chunk j hold tokens 4j\'854j+3. Then:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The core state u_j exists once token 4j+3 has been read.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The decoder at position t predicts x_\{t+1\}, so it may use u_j only if 4j+3 \uc0\u8804  t.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The latest chunk it can use is j*(t) = 
\f5 \uc0\u8970 
\f1 (t+1)/4
\f5 \uc0\u8971 
\f1  \uc0\u8722  1.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
u_\{j*\} predicts chunk j*+1, which is exactly the chunk that contains x_\{t+1\}. This matches NCP's shift.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Test:
\f1\b0  editing token 101 first changes the plan at t = 103, the end of its chunk, and never earlier. So the decoder can't copy its target through the core.\

\f3\b Bug 1 (caught by the test):
\f1\b0  NCP's shift formula gives a zero ("null") plan only at the start of the row. With packed rows, the first 3 tokens of every later document were reading the previous document's last concept. 
\f3\b Fix:
\f1\b0  use the null plan whenever chunk j* belongs to another document.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Step 2: What the NCP loss really learns\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 For any prediction \uc0\u265 :\
$$\\mathbb\{E\}|\\hat c-c|^2=|\\hat c-\\mu|^2+\\operatorname\{tr\}\\operatorname\{Cov\}(c\\mid\\text\{context\}),\\qquad \\mu=\\mathbb\{E\}[c\\mid\\text\{context\}]$$\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The second term doesn't depend on \uc0\u265 , so MSE is minimised by \u265  = \u956 , the 
\f3\b conditional mean
\f1\b0 .\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Your M1 measurement (R\'b2 = 3.6%) says \uc0\u956  is close to the average concept. Most of the next chunk simply can't be predicted.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The deeper problem: with 128 codes in 8 dimensions, many different \uc0\u960  give the same average. So under MSE, \u960  carries no meaning. The "concept vocabulary" is only a set of weights for averaging.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Proposed change:
\f1\b0  cross-entropy on the code indices, as next-token prediction does for tokens:\
$$\\mathcal L_\{\\rm NCP\}=-\\frac1S\\sum_s\\log\\pi^s!\\left[n^s_\{m+1\}\\right],\\qquad n^s=\\arg\\min_n|c^s-e^s_n|$$\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Here S is the number of segments, \uc0\u960 
\f4 \uc0\u738 
\f1  is segment s's predicted distribution over its codes, and n
\f4 \uc0\u738 
\f1 _\{m+1\} is the index of the code nearest to segment s of the next concept.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Its unique optimum is \uc0\u960  = the true conditional distribution over codes, so each \u960  now means something.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The decoder still receives \uc0\u265  = \u931 \u960 \'b7sg(e). That is essentially the same average vector, but each \u960  is now a real distribution, with an entropy the model can use and a vocabulary that VQ-only steering can adjust.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The paper's MSE stays as an ablation arm.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Step 3: The vocabulary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Size.
\f1\b0  64 segments \'d7 128 codes \'d7 8 dimensions = 512 = d_c. That's 448 bits per concept, against 52 bits for the identities of its 4 tokens. The 8-dimension segment shape is the one DARWINMINDS measured at 68% variance explained.\

