\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 1.1 What arrives at the concept module?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The frozen encoder hands over one vector per chunk of 4 tokens:\
$$\\hat c_m \\in \\mathbb\{R\}^\{d_c\},\\qquad |\\hat c_m| = \\sqrt\{d_c\},\\qquad m = 1,\\dots,M,\\quad M = T/4$$\
Every symbol:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
m
\f1\b0  is the chunk index. Chunk m holds tokens 4m\uc0\u8722 3 \'85 4m, counting tokens from 1.\
\ls20\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
d_c
\f1\b0  is the concept width (512 on the M2).\
\ls20\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f7 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8214 
\f3 \uc0\u265 _m
\f7 \uc0\u8214 
\f3  = \uc0\u8730 d_c
\f1\b0  means every concept lies on a sphere of that radius. Only its direction carries information; its size can't drift.\
\ls20\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
T
\f1\b0  is the row length (1024 tokens), so M = 256 concepts per row.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 What each \uc0\u265 _m contains is a learned blend of three sources:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
e
\f8 \uc0\u8320 
\f1\b0 , the raw token embeddings: which exact tokens are in the chunk.\
\ls21\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
L
\f1\b0 , the local stream: context from at most 63 tokens back.\
\ls21\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
G
\f1\b0 , the global stream: the two FULL layers have already looked across the whole past.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 It is 
\f3\b causal
\f1\b0 : \uc0\u265 _m depends only on tokens 1 \'85 4m. Your encoder test checks this bit for bit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 1.2 What can the module do that nothing else can?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is the central question, and your whole failure ledger (F1\'96F7) says the answer decides whether the module gets used.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 First, what the decoder already has.
\f1\b0  It sees the local stream L, which reaches 63 tokens back. Write:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
H(A | B)
\f1\b0 , conditional entropy: the best achievable loss, in nats, for predicting A when you know B.\
\ls22\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
I(A ; B | C)
\f1\b0 , conditional mutual information: how much B tells you about A beyond what C already told you.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A decoder limited to the last R = 63 tokens can reach loss H(x_\{t+1\} | x_\{t\uc0\u8722 62..t\}) at best. With the whole past it could reach H(x_\{t+1\} | x_\{\u8804 t\}). The chain rule gives the gap exactly:\
$$\\Delta_R = I\\big(x_\{t+1\};\\ x_\{<t-62\}\\ \\big|\\ x_\{t-62..t\}\\big) \\ \\ge 0$$\
In words, \uc0\u916 _R is what the far past tells you about the next token that the last 63 tokens don't. 
\f3\b The concept module is the only channel that can deliver \uc0\u916 _R to the decoder.
\f1\b0  That is its first job.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 The catch:
\f1\b0  a module that does nothing still passes \uc0\u265  along, and \u265  already contains G, the encoder's global retrieval. So an identity module already delivers part of \u916 _R for free. The real question is what the module adds 
\f9\i beyond passing \uc0\u265  through
\f1\i0 . There are three candidate jobs:\

