\f3\b \cf0 Why the encoder's FULL layers give about 2 hops.
\f1\b0  One FULL layer is one content lookup: a query matches a key and copies its value. Because the encoder has a convolution on Q, K and V, each key already contains its predecessor. So one layer can complete a full induction step, "find where X occurred before and read what came after it". Two FULL layers therefore give roughly two such hops.\
So the module's 
\f3\b unique
\f1\b0  jobs are J2 (more hops than the encoder does) and J3 (forward prediction at the chunk level). J1 comes along for free. This matters for Step 4: any computation inside the module that only duplicates the encoder's two hops will go unused, which is F7 again.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 1.3 Causality: when may the decoder read the module?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Let the module's output at chunk j be u_j, built from \uc0\u265 _1 \'85 \u265 _j. Counting tokens from 0, chunk j holds tokens 4j \'85 4j+3.\
The decoder at position t predicts x_\{t+1\}. It may read u_j only if u_j contains nothing from x_\{t+1\} onward. u_j has seen tokens up to 4j+3, so we need\
$$4j + 3 \\le t \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad j^*(t) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac\{t+1\}\{4\} \\right\\rfloor - 1$$\
