\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 1.5 The gradient contract\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 There are three gradient paths, each forced:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Module input \uc0\u265  without stop-gradient.
\f1\b0  The encoder learns what to pool only from the gradient that comes back through the module. Otherwise the concepts are shaped by nothing downstream.\
\ls24\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The NCP target is stop-gradient: sg(c_\{m+1\}).
\f1\b0  Here is why, from scratch. If the target could move, the loss 
\f4 \uc0\u8214 
\f1 prediction \uc0\u8722  c_\{m+1\}
\f4 \uc0\u8214 
\f1 \'b2 has a trivial minimum: the encoder makes every concept the same vector, so prediction becomes perfect and the concepts carry nothing. This is the representation collapse that JEPA-style methods guard against. The stop-gradient removes that escape. It is also your frozen encoder rule.\
\ls24\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
NTP reaches the module through the plan.
\f1\b0  This is the module's main teacher. NCP is an auxiliary signal on top of it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 1.6 The budget rule\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The module runs on M = T/4 positions. A layer of width d_c costs about 12\'b7d_c\'b2 multiply-adds per position, so relative to one encoder token layer of width d it costs\
$$\\frac\{12,d_c^2 \\cdot T/4\}\{12,d^2 \\cdot T\} = \\frac\{1\}\{4\}\\left(\\frac\{d_c\}\{d\}\\right)^2$$\
At d_c = d, 
\f3\b 4 module layer applications cost the same as 1 encoder layer.
\f1\b0  The module can afford depth or looping cheaply. The actual amount is Step 4.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

