\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Mean pooling from first principles\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Stack a chunk's 4 token states into one vector of size 4d. Mean pooling is then a fixed linear map:\
$$c = W_\{\\text\{mean\}\},[h_1; h_2; h_3; h_4],\\qquad W_\{\\text\{mean\}\} = \\tfrac14,[,I\\ \\ I\\ \\ I\\ \\ I,]$$\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls29\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
What it keeps:
\f1\b0  the component of the chunk along the "all four equal" direction. It projects the 4d-dimensional chunk onto a d-dimensional subspace.\
\ls29\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
What it throws away:
\f1\b0  everything in the other 3d dimensions, i.e. every pattern where the four states sum to zero.\
\ls29\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It is order-blind.
\f1\b0  Swap h
\f6 \uc0\u8321 
\f1  and h
\f6 \uc0\u8322 
\f1  and c doesn't change, so a chunk becomes a bag of states.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Why that's usually fine.
\f1\b0  The encoder is causal, so each h_i already contains its own prefix. h
\f6 \uc0\u8324 
\f1  has seen the whole chunk, and h
\f6 \uc0\u8322 
\f1  knows what came before it. Order is carried 
\f9\i inside
\f1\i0  the states, so averaging loses little of it.\

\f3\b Where it does lose something.
\f1\b0  The raw embedding tap e
\f6 \uc0\u8320 
\f1  has no position information. Averaging four embeddings gives a bag of tokens, so "dog bites man" and "man bites dog" within one chunk give the same e
\f6 \uc0\u8320 
\f1  contribution. Order must then come entirely from the L and G parts. Your IHN result (nested_scopes 0.05 vs 1.00) shows that exact identity and order are what the core tends to wash out.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Where your frozen encoder stands\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Encoder v1.0 uses\
$$c_m = W_\{\\text\{pool\}\},[m_\{4m-3\};\\dots;m_\{4m\}],\\qquad W_\{\\text\{pool\}\} \\text\{ initialised to \} \\tfrac14[I\\ I\\ I\\ I]$$\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls30\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
At step 0 it is exactly NCP's mean pooling.
\f1\b0  The only differences are that it is applied to the depth-mix m_t rather than the last layer alone, and that it is followed by the sphere norm. Your 
\f2\fs26 encoder_v1.py
\f1\fs24  test confirms the mean-pooling init.\
\ls30\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Training moves it away from the mean only if that lowers the next-token loss.
\f1\b0  So "learned, initialised to the mean" can do no worse at the start and can recover the discarded subspace when it's useful, for example to keep token order within the chunk.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Scalability of the learned version:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls31\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The parameter count is 4d\'b7d_c: about 1M at d = 512, and 67M at d = d_c = 4096.\
\ls31\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The compute per token is d\'b7d_c multiply-adds, against about 12d\'b2 for one transformer layer. That's roughly 8% of one layer at any width.\
\ls31\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
So it scales as well as mean pooling in practice. The extra cost is a small, constant fraction of one layer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

