\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Q1: Recurrence \'97 a loop, HRM or TRM?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 What NCP did:
\f1\b0  a plain stack of causal transformer layers over concepts (8 layers at 8.9B, 4 at 1B). No recurrence.\

\f3\b What recurrence buys, from scratch.
\f1\b0  Each attention layer can do one content lookup: find the concept that matches, and copy it. A chain like "A 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  B, then B 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  C" needs one lookup after another, so it needs sequential depth:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls34\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A stack of N layers gives N sequential lookups and costs N layers of parameters.\
\ls34\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A block of B layers applied R times gives B\'b7R lookups and costs only B layers of parameters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 So recurrence buys 
\f3\b depth without parameters
\f1\b0 . It does 
\f9\i not
\f1\i0  buy depth without FLOPs: at equal FLOPs, an untied stack usually matches or beats a loop on plain language loss, because it has more parameters. A loop earns its place in two cases: tasks that need more sequential steps (composition, and hop counts beyond those seen in training), and a smaller model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

