\f1\b0  1M tokens becomes 256k concepts, so move to Kimi-style 3:1 linear:FULL (GDN-2 : gated MLA) inside the module. The state-tracking ability it adds belongs here, because this is where composition happens.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Layer anatomy:
\f1\b0  reuse your encoder's FULL layer unchanged, at concept rate. One block design everywhere.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls38\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Gated attention:
\f1\b0  y = W_O(SDPA 
\f10 \uc0\u8857 
\f1  \uc0\u963 (W_\u952  x)). The sigmoid gate lets a head output nothing, so it needs no attention sink.\
\ls38\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
QKV short conv, kernel 4 over concepts, reset at document boundaries.
\f1\b0  Each key then already contains its predecessor, so one layer can do concept-level induction: "find the concept that matches mine and read 
\f9\i the one after it
\f1\i0 ". This is the same argument as in the encoder, one level up.\
\ls38\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
NoPE
\f1\b0  (no positional encoding) with a per-head temperature \uc0\u964 _h (no weight decay), per-head QK-norm, and GQA with 8 query and 2 KV heads. Order comes from the causal mask and the conv.\
\ls38\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
SwiGLU FFN
\f1\b0 , hidden size 1408. MoE at scale.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Q3: The residual scheme\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 What a residual must do inside a loop:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls39\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Carry the state with an identity path.
\f1\b0  Through the loop, \uc0\u8706 out/\u8706 in = \u8719 _k (I + A_k). The I in each factor prevents vanishing gradients.\
\ls39\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Limit the gain per step.
\f1\b0  
\f4 \uc0\u8214 
\f1 \uc0\u8719 (I + A_k)
\f4 \uc0\u8214 
\f1  \uc0\u8804  exp(\u931 
\f4 \uc0\u8214 
\f1 A_k
\f4 \uc0\u8214 
\f1 ), so large branches can explode.\
\ls39\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Counter dilution.
\f1\b0  In deep stacks each layer's share of the stream shrinks like 1/\uc0\u8730 depth.\
\ls39\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Give a clean "off" switch
\f1\b0  for ablations.\
\ls39\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Match the encoder,
\f1\b0  so the whole network uses one block grammar.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 How big is the risk here?
\f1\b0  The loop has 6 layer applications, and only the 2-layer block is repeated. So the product has 6 factors, the same as a plain 6-layer stack. Repeating shared weights twice only squares the gain of the repeated part, which isn't dangerous at R = 2. 
\f3\b No special brake is needed.
\f1\b0  It would become necessary around R \uc0\u8805  4, which is the regime of HRM-Text's 35.7\'d7 gradient spikes.\

