\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 2. Is a stochastic concept (GRAM) a good choice? Derived from scratch\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 What a latent variable is for.
\f1\b0  Take N-Queens with two valid boards, A and B, equally likely. A deterministic model that predicts every cell 
\f9\i independently
\f1\i0  learns, for each cell,\
$$p(y_i)=\\tfrac12[\\text\{A's value\}]+\\tfrac12[\\text\{B's value\}]$$\
Taking the most likely value per cell then mixes the two boards, and the mixture is usually invalid. GRAM fixes this with a latent z that first 
\f9\i picks
\f1\i0  a board, and then decodes all cells consistently with it:\
$$p(y\\mid x)=\\sum_z p(z\\mid x)\\prod_i p(y_i\\mid z,x)$$\

\f3\b Our decoder is autoregressive, so it doesn't have this problem.
\f1\b0  It predicts tokens one at a time, each conditioned on the ones before:\
$$p(x_\{t+1..t+4\}\\mid\\text\{ctx\})=\\prod_\{i=1\}^\{4\}p(x_\{t+i\}\\mid x_\{<t+i\},\\text\{ctx\})$$\
This chain rule is exact. Once the first token commits to a mode, every later token conditions on that choice. So a deterministic plan plus an autoregressive decoder can already represent any distribution over continuations. The latent adds no expressive power.\

\f3\b So the latent will collapse.
\f1\b0  The training objective is\
$$\\log p(y\\mid x)\\ \\ge\\ \\mathbb\{E\}_\{q\}\\big[\\log p(y\\mid z,x)\\big]-\\mathrm\{KL\}\\big(q(z\\mid x,y),|,p(z\\mid x)\\big)$$\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls45\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
y
\f1\b0  is the target, 
\f3\b z
\f1\b0  the latent, 
\f3\b q
\f1\b0  the posterior, which sees y, and 
\f3\b p(z | x)
\f1\b0  the prior used at inference.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls45\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If the decoder already reaches the full likelihood without z, then any informative q pays KL > 0 and buys nothing.\
\ls45\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The optimum is therefore q = p: the latent is ignored. This is the known posterior collapse of VAE language models (Bowman et al., 2016).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls45\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You already measured it:
\f1\b0  in PRISM, K latent candidates collapsed to one vector (cos \uc0\u8805  0.999999) in 10 of 10 cells.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Two practical hazards on top:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls46\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A leak from the future.
\f1\b0  GRAM's posterior needs the target. For us that's the next chunk, sg(c_\{m+1\}). During training the plan could smuggle the answer in through \uc0\u949 , limited only by \u946 \'b7KL, and GRAM uses \u946  = 0.04\'960.5. The training loss is then no longer a true likelihood, and your switch-off tests become noisy.\
\ls46\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The machinery.
\f1\b0  A posterior network, KL balancing, 16 supervision steps and halting. The authors name this as their scaling barrier.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Verdict: no stochastic concepts in pretraining.
\f1\b0  GRAM fits a later stage: fine-tuning on verifiable tasks with several valid answers, using best-of-N with a value head. The natural insertion point is the loop state between passes (h = u + \uc0\u949 ), so the design keeps that point open. If you ever want cheap exploration, both GRAM (noise alone: 94.9 on Sudoku) and the diffusion paper show that plain noise at inference, with no posterior network, gets most of it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 3. DiscoLoop: adopt it at the concept level\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 The problem, derived.
\f1\b0  The shared block B sees two different input distributions:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls47\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Pass 1:
\f1\b0  clean inputs, built from \uc0\u265 .\
\ls47\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Pass 2:
\f1\b0  its own pass-1 output, which is noisy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 DiscoLoop measured exactly this. The bridge was decodable with probability 1.00, but the state's cosine with the bridge embedding was only about 0.3. Pass 2 therefore queries its memory with a smeared key. Snapping the state back toward the input vocabulary fixes it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Our version needs no new parts.
\f1\b0  The NCP head and the concept codebook already form a soft decode followed by a re-encode. And the codebook is fitted to sg(\uc0\u265 ), the loop's own input distribution. That makes it the concept-level equivalent of tied embeddings. After pass 1, at every concept position m:\
$$\\pi^s_\{m,n\}=\\mathrm\{softmax\}
\f9\i n!\\Big(-\\tfrac\{|(W_q,\\mathrm\{zcRMS\}(R^1_m))^s-\\mathrm\{sg\}(e^s_n)|^2\}\{\\tau_s\}\\Big),\\qquad \\Phi(R^1_m)=\\big[\\textstyle\\sum_n \\pi^s
\f1\i0 \{m,n\},\\mathrm\{sg\}(e^s_n)\\big]_\{s=1..S\}$$\
$$R^1_m \\leftarrow R^1_m+\\alpha\\cdot\\mathrm\{RMSNorm\}\\big(\\Phi(R^1_m)\\big),\\qquad \\alpha = 1\\ \\text\{(fixed)\}$$\
Every symbol:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls48\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
R\'b9_m
\f1\b0  is the stream after pass 1.\
\ls48\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
s
\f1\b0  indexes the S segments of the concept vector, and 
\f3\b n
\f1\b0  the codes within segment s.\
\ls48\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
W_q
\f1\b0  is the NCP head's query projection, and 
\f3\b e^s_n
\f1\b0  the codewords.\
\ls48\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u960 
\f1\b0  is the head's belief over codes, with temperature 
\f3\b \uc0\u964 _s
\f1\b0 .\
\ls48\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u934 
\f1\b0  is the belief-weighted average codeword. Geometrically, it's a point inside the convex hull of each segment's codes: when the head is confident, \uc0\u934  \u8776  one clean codeword (a snap onto the concept vocabulary); when unsure, \u934  \u8776  the average.\
\ls48\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
RMSNorm
\f1\b0  brings \uc0\u934  to the stream's scale (norm \u8730 d_c).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The channel is causal, since \uc0\u934  at position m uses only R\'b9_m. Its cost is about 0.6 MFLOP per token, which is negligible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Why it failed in your Q-LoopNCP, and what's different now:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls49\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The learned gate closed
\f1\b0  (0.119 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  0.01, the saturated-sigmoid trap). DiscoLoop's cleanest wins used a 
\f9\i fixed
\f1\i0  \uc0\u945  = 1, so we use \u945  = 1. The channel can't be switched off; pass 2 learns how much to rely on it.\
\ls49\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The codebook was weak
\f1\b0  (32 codes per 32 dimensions; codebooks like it had 68\'9679% dead codes). \uc0\u934  is only as good as the codebook, which makes Step 2 more important than before.\
\ls49\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Caveat:
\f1\b0  DiscoLoop's evidence is for facts stored in the weights. Your pointer chasing retrieves facts from the context. The mismatch argument applies to both, because pass 2's query must still match clean keys, but that's untested.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 It forces one more decision: no deep supervision on pass 1.
\f1\b0  DiscoLoop's signature of real composition is that pass-1 accuracy on two-hop 
\f9\i collapses
\f1\i0  while test accuracy jumps: pass 1 holds the bridge, and pass 2 produces the answer. QCR-NCP showed the same split (pass 1 held the bridge with probe 1.00, pass 2 the answer). Supervising pass 1 toward the 
\f9\i final
\f1\i0  next concept would reward the single-pass shortcut. So the NCP loss is applied only after the last pass. The head still learns from pass 1 through the next-token gradient that flows through \uc0\u934 .\

\f3\b It replaces last turn's per-pass re-injection of \uc0\u265 .
\f1\b0  With R = 2 the loop can't forget its input. The useful thing to inject between passes is the decoded intermediate, not the input again.\

\f3\b Budget caveat:
\f1\b0  DiscoLoop's language-loss gain appeared after about 13B tokens. On the M2 (about 100M tokens), judge it on the hop tasks, where its gains came within a few hundred epochs, not on language loss.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 4. The diffusion paper: what transfers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 It confirms three choices we already made:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls50\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The carried state does the computation.
\f1\b0  Without the hidden state the model was worse; resetting it dropped Sudoku to 58%. So: carried state, no reset.\
\ls50\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
No loop-index input.
\f1\b0  It worked without any timestep input, so our shared block needs no pass-index embedding.\
\ls50\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Separate norms for what is carried and what is read out.
\f1\b0  It uses LN_h and LN_o; ours are the stream R and the readout zcRMS(R).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 What doesn't transfer:
\f1\b0  noise-driven "anytime" solving. It needs unique-solution constraint tasks, thousands of steps and an annealed-noise training curriculum. Our module is a causal language model with R = 2. If you later want test-time depth, the route is to train with a sampled R (its K ~ Unif[20, 160]), which is a scale-phase option.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

