The full loop does use recurrence.
\f1\b0  One pass gives 4.4%. But it learned about 2\'d7 slower, because the same 3 layers must serve two different input distributions (pass 1 sees concepts, pass 2 sees its own output).\
\ls58\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
No loop extrapolates.
\f1\b0  A third pass doesn't solve 3 hops (\uc0\u8804  7%). Trained with a fixed R = 2, the loop learned "a 2-hop circuit", not "one more hop per pass". The papers that get extrapolation (Loop-Think-Generalize; the diffusion paper, with K ~ Unif[20, 160]) 
\f3\b sample the number of passes during training
\f1\b0 . That is the missing ingredient if recurrence is to buy test-time depth.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 How much of the whole network recurs.
\f1\b0  Rough estimate, since the decoder isn't designed yet (I assumed about 6 SWA layers):\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls59\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
encoder about 165 MFLOP per token, v1 concept module about 29, decoder about 124: about 320 in total;\
\ls59\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
the looped block is about 6% of FLOPs, and the 
\f9\i repeated
\f1\i0  pass is about 
\f3\b 3%
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 So yes: in v1, recurrence is a sliver of the network, and the test shows that sliver is idle.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Step 3: DiscoLoop \'97 not working in our setting\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 In-context:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls60\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It 
\f3\b slowed
\f1\b0  the subset loop in both seeds (+750 steps each).\
\ls60\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It 
\f3\b sped up
\f1\b0  the full loop (6,750 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  5,250 steps, one seed). That is the one setting where the loop was genuinely needed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 In-weight (about 700 epochs):
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls61\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
No arm generalised. ID was 15\'9627%, OOD \uc0\u8804  1.5%, and the untied stack was best (27%). So this test sits 
\f9\i before
\f1\i0  the phase transition where DiscoLoop's own gains appeared.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls61\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The mechanism broke.
\f1\b0  DiscoLoop needs the pass-1 state to decode the bridge. Without the channel it does (35% in SUB, 62% in FULL). With the channel, every variant stopped decoding it (2\'965%) and instead decoded other codes confidently (93\'9696%).\
\ls61\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
I ruled out my own setup.
\f1\b0  An exact-vocabulary head behaved the same (3.8%). DiscoLoop's learned gate didn't close (it settled at 0.43) and behaved the same too (3.6%).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 First-principles reading.
\f1\b0  The head is supervised only at the final output, so the pass-1 state is unconstrained. A channel read from an unconstrained state becomes whatever message minimises the loss. Nothing forces it to mean "the bridge". DiscoLoop works when bridge decodability has already emerged before the channel matters (long training in its setting); from scratch, the model co-adapts the channel away. 
\f3\b Verdict:
\f1\b0  drop it from the default. Keep it only as an arm on a loop that's actually used, where it was the one positive result.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

