\f3\b \cf0 What this shows:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls67\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Exactly one hop per pass.
\f1\b0  With v2's final weights, R passes solve exactly R hops: R = 2 gives hop 2 at 99.9% and hop 3 at 13%; R = 3 gives hop 3 at 99.9% and hop 4 at 18%. The loop is the computation, which v1's loop never was.\
\ls67\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It extrapolates to twice the training depth,
\f1\b0  with 3.4\'d7 fewer parameters than NCP's stack, and it learned faster.\
\ls67\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Extra passes don't hurt.
\f1\b0  Hops 1\'964 stay at 99.7\'96100% with 8 passes.\
\ls67\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Sampling R is not what creates extrapolation.
\f1\b0  Fixed-R training also extrapolates. The two ingredients are one shallow block per pass, and the input re-read on the only path. Sampling kept the result more stable late in training (final hop 6: 77% vs 31%) and halves training compute (2.5 passes on average vs 4).\
\ls67\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Honest caveat:
\f1\b0  extrapolation drifts during training. Both v2 runs peaked mid-training (96.9% and 99.2% on hop 6), so checkpoint selection matters. A second layer per pass also works, but learned more slowly (hop 4 peaked at 95%).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

