\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 Step 2: Explorative Modeling as the z for GRAM (hypothesis 2)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Why GRAM failed our earlier analysis.
\f1\b0  Its latent is trained through a VAE posterior q(z | x, y) with a KL penalty. Two problems follow: the posterior sees the future, and an autoregressive decoder can ignore z, which leads to collapse. 
\f3\b XM removes the posterior entirely.
\f1\b0  With K learned latents z
\f6 \uc0\u8321 
\f1  \'85 z_K, it trains only on the candidate closest to the target:\
$$\\mathcal L=\\min_k J\\big(\\hat y(z_k),,y\\big)$$\
Here J is the loss of candidate k (for us, the NCP loss) and y is the target. Different z_k can then commit to different futures. The latent is found by search, not by an encoder, so there's no KL term and no posterior. So your connection is right: XM is a way to get GRAM's multiple hypotheses without the VAE.\

\f3\b Where it should help, from first principles.
\f1\b0  XM helps where one prediction must describe 
\f9\i several future parts at once
\f1\i0  with a factorised or regression loss. That is exactly NCP: one concept per 4 future tokens, over 64 independent code segments. Plain autoregressive token prediction needs it least, which is also what the XM paper found.\

\f3\b One rule the design must obey.
\f1\b0  The winning index k* = argmin_k J_k is computed from the future. If the decoder read the 
\f9\i winning
\f1\i0  candidate's plan during training, up to log
\f6 \uc0\u8322 
\f1 K bits about the future would leak into next-token prediction. So during training the decoder reads a candidate chosen at random; the NCP loss alone uses the winner.\

