The loop grokked 2-hop between steps 6,000 and 7,500. 
\f3\b But its accuracy is identical from 1 to 16 passes, and its state grows by the same vector on every pass
\f1\b0  (S_r = r\'b7v, step 0.404). So the 2 hops come from the encoder's FULL layers, which pull hop 1 into the query's concept, plus 
\f9\i one
\f1\i0  core pass. The recurrence isn't used yet, and hop 3, which would need it, wasn't reached in the budget.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Is that a trap or a phase?
\f1\b0  At concept level I trained the same loop on 1-hop first, which puts it into this ignore-the-state regime. Within 500 steps of multi-hop data it used its state again, and with 8 passes it reached 99% on hop 4 and 95% on hop 5 (trained on 1\'963). So it's a phase, not a trap: the loop engages when the data demands depth. End to end, the demand arrives later, because the encoder already covers the first hop. That's exactly Step 1's prediction.\

\f3\b Chunk straddling
\f1\b0  (3-token facts in 4-token chunks) makes 1-hop retrieval about 3\'d7 slower to learn, but it isn't what limits composition: with aligned facts, 2-hop is the same at step 4,500 (16% vs 23% straddled).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

